// docs/spec/en/07-constexpr-and-consteval.md (ch09) §9.1(5) forbids
// `consteval` only on "a destructor, allocation function, or deallocation
// function". An overloaded operator function is none of those, so it may be
// declared `consteval`; §9.1(1)/(2) and front-matter §1(2) adopt
// [dcl.constexpr]/1-2 unchanged, and those permit it too.
// §9.1(4) then requires the operator call -- which is a call to an immediate
// function, spelled with operator notation ([over.match.oper]) -- to produce
// a constant expression. Both operands are named `constexpr` objects, so the
// implicit object argument and the explicit argument are both constant.
// docs/spec/en/06-constant-evaluation.md (ch06) §7.2(6.2) requires the
// implementation to support the call.
//
// Call position: block scope, written in operator notation. Callee:
// `consteval` overloaded operator. Receiver: named object.
//
// Expected value derived from the spec text above and independently confirmed
// with `clang++-22 -std=c++26`, which compiles this exact source and exits 42.
//
// CURRENT STATUS (scpp @ a59ffce): FAILS -- but not because `operator+` is
// missing: scpp resolves it to `Offset_operator_plus` and then reports
// "error: internal error: no generated code for resolved function
// 'Offset_operator_plus' called here", i.e. the immediate operator call is
// lowered as a runtime call instead of being folded. The expectation above
// comes from the spec and from clang; do not weaken it to match the
// implementation.
class Offset {
public:
    virtual ~Offset() = default;
public:
    int value{};

    constexpr Offset(int v) {
        this->value = v;
        return;
    }

    consteval int operator+(const Offset& other) const {
        return this->value + other.value;
    }
};

int main() {
    constexpr Offset a{20};
    constexpr Offset b{22};
    return a + b;
}
